# Purpose
Explains SlopGuard in plain words: what AI slop is, why AI produces it, the
ten checks every turn runs before it claims a result, the independent check
for substantive work, and what the human can do with the result.

# Key Sections
- What AI slop is (one-paragraph definition covering prose, code, UI and agent work).
- Why it happens (model side and process side).
- The ten SlopGuard checks, each with a one-line test.
- What you see in greatVibe (the SlopGuard line on result cards).
- What you can do as the human.
- Sources (public URLs only).

# Dependencies
- `what-is-ace.md`: SlopGuard is described as the output-side partner of ACE.
- `../how-to/review-ai-work.md`: the human review guide this page points to.
- The engineering spec lives in the private docs library; this page is a
  fresh public rewrite and must not link to it.

# Sanitization
Written fresh. No internal service names, private paths, tool names or
implementation contracts. Research citations are public URLs only.

# Gotchas
- Status: published 2026-10-26 with gvmesh 0.6.384 (always-on SlopGuard
  instruction and audit brief) and konui 373ef03d (SlopGuard space, Files
  badges). If shipped behaviour changes (check names, card wording), update
  this page first.
- The ten check names and order must match the engineering spec (SG1-SG10).
- Keep brand voice: no filler words, no competitor names, no em-dash
  trailing qualifiers. This page is itself held to SlopGuard.

